VST3 Plugin Analyzer @VERSION@ for Linux (@ARCH@)

A lightweight VST3 effect analyzer inspired by Christian Budde's VST Plugin
Analyzer. A C++ host loads the plugin in a separate process; a Python/Qt interface
provides measurements, interactive plots, and parameter controls. No audio interface is required.

Contains Python source, a compiled C++ plugin host, and desktop assets.
Python, Qt and other third-party dependencies are NOT bundled. Plugins are separate.
Requires glibc >= @GLIBC@ and libstdc++ >= @STDCPP@ (GCC runtime package version).

INSTALL
Extract this archive and open a terminal in the extracted directory.
For your account:
  ./install.sh
For all users:
  sudo ./install.sh

The installer installs missing system libraries using APT (Ubuntu/Debian) or
DNF (Fedora). A per-user installation may request sudo for system libraries.
It uses compatible installed Python libraries when available; otherwise it
creates a private Python environment and downloads the required libraries.
An internet connection is needed to download missing dependencies.
System Python is not modified. Other distributions need their system libraries
and Python 3.10 or newer installed beforehand.

The application uses NumPy >=2,<3, PySide6 >=6.8,<7 and pyqtgraph >=0.13.7,<1.
Its private environment currently uses PySide6 below 6.12 for compatibility.
Plugins and any additional vendor-specific libraries are installed separately.

LAUNCH
Use the applications menu, or run:
  ~/.local/bin/vst3-plugin-analyzer
For an all-user installation:
  /usr/local/bin/vst3-plugin-analyzer

UNINSTALL
For your account:
  ~/.local/lib/vst3-plugin-analyzer/uninstall.sh
For all users:
  sudo /usr/local/lib/vst3-plugin-analyzer/uninstall.sh
The archive's ./uninstall.sh also works: run without sudo for your account,
or with sudo to remove the all-user installation.

Uninstallation removes the application and its managed Python environment.
Settings, plugins, system libraries and separately installed Python environments
are preserved. User installations are under ~/.local; all-user installations
are under /usr/local. Remove an existing source/package-manager installation
before switching to a system-wide tar installation. To update a tar installation,
close the application and run the new archive's installer in the same mode.
